<<~ ahu slot=`#/head`>>

! Graceful Parsing ~ all of TiddlyWiki on a gradient

''The principle (operator, 2026-06-27):'' scale "parse on a GRADIENT, degrade GRACEFULLY" to ALL of
TiddlyWiki5 parsing, beyond the `<<~` memetic sigils. No parse SHALL break badly: every parse
yields a best-effort tree; malformation rides the tree as graded nodes, never a throw, never a
confidently-wrong structure. A five-spirit research swarm (2026-06-27) found the field converging on
this model with rare unanimity. This carrier holds the design; each section marks what stands in
code and what stands UNBUILT.

The house invents nothing here: it NAMES what the island grammar and its Water half-do, and FINISHES it.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-tower`>>

!! The superset tower ~ HTML ⊂ TW5 wikitext ⊂ memetic-wikitext

The grammar forms a NESTED SUPERSET TOWER, each layer a strict superset of the one below, and the
WHOLE stack degrades on the same gradient:

* ''HTML'' — the base. TW5 wikitext parses raw HTML inline (the core `html.js` · `entity.js`
  · `commentblock.js` · `commentinline.js` wikirules), so ''TW5 wikitext ⊃ HTML''.
* ''TW5 wikitext'' ⊃ HTML — and memetic INHERITS it: the `MemeticParser` subclass filters the core
  rule classes by a deny-list that defaults EMPTY (`DEFAULT_RULES_EXCEPT` in `memetic-parser.ts`), so
  every core rule (incl. `html`) fires in a memetic doc.
* ''memetic-wikitext'' ⊃ TW5 wikitext (lock 10: the `<<~` overlay, disjoint-match) — an ISLAND
  GRAMMAR that panics-to-water.

''The never-fail FLOOR (the deep elegance).'' Panic-to-water at the memetic layer yields VERBATIM
TEXT; verbatim text renders as escaped HTML text; ''HTML text always renders.'' So water falls all the
way down the tower to HTML-text, which cannot fail — the tower has a GUARANTEED never-fail ground.
Every malformation, however severe, degrades to "verbatim bytes shown as text," never a crash. The
gradient and its floor stand structural, not aspirational.

''HTML5 stands as the canonical exemplar.'' The HTML5 parsing algorithm (tag-soup → a valid DOM,
explicit error-recovery for every malformation, a browser that NEVER fails to parse) sets the gold
standard for a never-fail parser — the property the whole tower emulates upward. The tower's work:
TW5 wikitext and memetic-wikitext come to match the never-fail guarantee HTML holds at the base.

''No HTML5 library sits at the floor.'' The TW5-native parser degrades without loss on its own (a
malformed open tag reads as literal text; an unclosed element keeps every byte and runs on to the end
of the tiddler), and "water → verbatim text → HTML text always renders" gives the never-fail floor with no
dependency. The source carries no `parse5` dependency and no HTML5 tag module. HTML5-era behaviours
(modern attribute grammar, foreign content, custom elements) belong in `html.js` itself, in the fork
this house owns: UNBUILT.

''Why a generic HTML parser cannot govern the tag grammar.'' TW5's `html` wikirule reads
WIKITEXT-AWARE: its `parse()` reads an element's children as wikitext (`parseBlocks`,
`parseInlineRun`), so `<div>''bold'' and a <$widget/></div>` interleaves HTML + wikitext + widgets. A
whole-subtree HTML5 pass swallows that interior as opaque HTML and DESTROYS widget invocation. TW5's
`html`-rule attributes THEMSELVES form a superset of HTML5 attributes: they interleave WIKITEXT into
attribute VALUES — `attr={{transclusion}}` (indirect) · `attr={{{filter}}}` (filtered) ·
`attr=<<macro>>` (macro) · bare `attr`→`value:"true"` (per `test-wikitext-parser.js`). A pure HTML5
parser holds no `{{}}`/`<<>>` grammar. Three measurements against parse5 (2026-06-27): it flattens
`attr={{X}}` to `value:"{{X}}"`, BREAKING transclusion/macro/filter attributes; it stays BLIND to
`$`-widgets (`$` opens no legal HTML name); and it CANNOT even find tag BOUNDARIES — an unquoted
`<<macro>>` value's `>` ends the tag early under the HTML5 unquoted-attribute-value state. So the live
rule keeps TW5's own `parseTag` and `parseAttribute`.

''What TW5's `html.js` lacks against HTML5'' (it holds NO insertion-mode state machine): real tag-soup
recovery (insertion modes + adoption agency), HTML5-complete foreign-content handling, and dedicated
raw-text/RCDATA handling (`<style>`/`<textarea>`/`<title>`). What it holds: the tag scanner accepts
hyphenated custom-element names and `<template>` · `<slot>` tags (`isLegalTag` rejects only a
leading-dash tag); `element.js` keeps hyphens (it strips every character outside `[0-9a-zA-Z-]` from a
tag name) and assigns the SVG and MathML namespaces.

''SECURITY AUDIT — the render gates and the holes they leave.'' TW5's only render gates: `script`→
`safe-script` (`element.js`, over `config.js` `htmlUnsafeElements` — `script` ALONE), the `on*`-strip
(`excludeEventAttributes` in `widget.js`, passed by `element.js`), and the tag-name clean to
`[0-9a-zA-Z-]` (`element.js`). OPEN HOLES TW5 leaves: `javascript:`/`vbscript:`/non-image-`data:`
schemes in URL-bearing attributes such as `href`, `src`, `xlink:href` and `action`; HTML content in
`srcdoc`; and `iframe`/`object`/`embed`/`base`/`form`/`frame*`/`noscript`. No sanitizer in this house
closes them. The allowlist sanitizer stands UNBUILT: a pure function with a tag allowlist, a per-tag
attribute allowlist, a URL-protocol check (dropping `javascript:`/`data:` except image data-URLs) and
`on*` removal, seeded from sanitize-html's default set, layered over TW5's render gates as defense in
depth. Its placement in a TW5-native parse path awaits an operator ruling. DOMPurify stays ruled out:
it needs a real DOM/`window` (not Worker-safe), and `isomorphic-dompurify` drags jsdom (heavy, plus a
second redundant parser).

''The PARSER loses nothing (deep-read in the fork, 2026-06-27).'' A malformed open tag → `parseTag`
returns null → `findNextTag` skips it → LITERAL TEXT (lossless). Where the expected `</tag>` never
appears, the element's body parses on through the end of the tiddler, past any ancestor's closer
(`parseBlocksTerminatedExtended` and `parseInlineRunTerminatedExtended` in `wikiparser.js`): no byte
drops, the rule records a `hint` diagnostic, `unterminated-html`, and the resulting nesting can remain
a guess. For a `<<` attribute value with `>>` ahead that fails macro parsing, `parseAttribute` returns
null (its guard in `parseutils.js`), so the enclosing tag fails to parse and never silently binds
`"true"`; without `>>` ahead, that guard does not fire.

''The parse receipt stands in the fork's core.'' `WikiParser` carries a `diagnostics` array
(`addDiagnostic`; each entry normalised by `$tw.utils.makeParseDiagnostic`): the rules that recover
silently record what they did — unterminated code block, inline code, emphasis, html, macro and
function definition, quote block, style block, typed block, conditional, hard line breaks. The block
driver catches a `RecoverableParseError`, keeps the block's source as a text node marked
`isRecovered`, records a diagnostic and resyncs at the next blank line, always advancing ≥1 char
(`recoverBlock`); no core rule throws that error at the pinned fork commit. `wiki.parseText` catches
the same error escaping a parser constructor and returns the error's tree, or the whole text as one
text node, with one diagnostic. The `parse-diagnostics` filter operator reads the array from the parse
cache (`grade` · `count` · `codes`).

''A finer `parseTag` rung — designed, UNBUILT.'' Recovery lives in the `parseTag` DRIVER;
`parseAttribute` (the value grammar) stays INTACT. The three driver changes (`html.js`):
# `parseTag` contract `node|null` → ''always-a-tree + diagnostics''. On a missing `>`: synthesize a
   zero-width ''MISSING `>`'', set `openTagEnd = pos`, keep the attrs parsed so far, emit a
   diagnostic. Recovery-set = next `>` | newline | `<`.
# Bad/unterminated tag-name: emit an ''Error node'' wrapping the `<…` span VERBATIM
   (`recoveredAs:"water"`, raw confidence), advance ''≥1 char'' (progress invariant).
# Attribute loop: when `parseAttribute` returns null on a malformed `<<` (it MUST NOT silently bind
   `"true"`), don't abort the tag — skip to the recovery-set, wrap the bad span as a graded
   attribute-error, continue under the ≥1-char invariant.

`parseAttribute` stays UNTOUCHED under that design — its extended value-branches (string/bare-
`"true"` · `{{indirect}}` · `{{{filtered}}}` · `<<macro>>` · `((mvv))` · `` `substituted` `` ·
unquoted) ride verbatim. MUST keep byte-exact: per-attr `start/end`; element `openTagStart/openTagEnd/
closeTagStart/closeTagEnd/rule:"html"/orderedAttributes` (set in `html.js` `parse()`, asserted by
`test-wikitext-parser.js`). Confidence shore: clean = `recoveredAs` absent / 20 · resynced =
`recoveredAs:"repaired"` mid · verbatim-wrapped = `recoveredAs:"water"` raw (water ships first).

''The integration GATE holds for any change to the live `html` rule:'' that rule dispatches EVERY
widget, so a change rides only with the fork's own suite green (`test-wikitext-parser.js` +
`test-wikitext.js` + widget specs, byte-exact node shapes incl.
`openTagStart/openTagEnd/closeTagStart/closeTagEnd/rule:"html"/orderedAttributes` + per-attr `start/end`
+ the extended-attribute types). Test vehicle: `node $TW/tiddlywiki.js ++packages/lararium-tw5/tiddlers
$TW/editions/test --test` (or the in-house `pnpm --filter @lararium/tw5 smoke:plugin-boot`).

''PRIOR ART (the field hand-writes the tag grammar + delegates values):'' MDX's
`micromark-extension-mdx-jsx` gives the near-exact analog (markdown-superset with `{expression}` in
attr values) — it HAND-WRITES the tag tokenizer and delegates ONLY the value-expression to acorn,
refusing a generic HTML parser for tags for //our// interleaving reason. TW5 holds this shape
(hand-written `parseTag` + `parseAttribute` delegating `{{}}`/`<<>>`). BurningTreeC's Lezer serves
editor-side reuse only. matklad's Resilient-LL supplies the recovery-set/≥1-char/Error-MISSING model.

''The break-badly sits at RENDER:'' a widget whose `render()`/`execute()` THROWS runs UNCONTAINED —
`renderChildren` (`widget.js`) loops `children[i].render()` with no try/catch, so one throwing
widget crashes the whole render tree. (An unknown widget degrades gracefully to `"Undefined widget"`
text; the gap covers //throwing// widgets.)

''The parser carries the recovery; the render gap stays open.'' The fork recovers at the PARSER: an
unmatched inline delimiter renders literally, an unclosed emphasis recovers, a block construct that
never terminates gets reported, each recovery stands visible in the parse tree, and the
`parse-diagnostics` filter reads them out. A per-widget render boundary (a try/catch around each child's
`render()` and `refresh()`, opt-in by a config flag) stands WITHDRAWN: its author backed it out in
favour of the parser recovery, and no branch of the fork carries it. A widget that throws at render
still takes its render tree down. Whoever closes that gap owes three answers the withdrawn design left
open: partial DOM a child inserted before it threw, a failure inside the error path itself, and the
cost of the check on a hot render.

''ERROR-SURFACING — TWO channels (operator: TW5 messages the user via `$:/alert` + system tags):''
(1) IN-PLACE — the inline `tc-error` span at the failure site (the gradient, in-render); (2) USER-
NOTIFICATION — a `$:/tags/Alert` tiddler so the user //knows// a degradation occurred (TW5's native
dismissible alert, same channel as the reboot-alert shore). CAVEAT: NEVER raise `$:/alert` from inside
`renderChildren` (mutating the wiki during render risks a re-render loop) — collect render-errors
in-render, raise the alert OUT-of-render (a hook/sweep, eventual-send), per the reboot-alert discipline.
Both channels stand UNBUILT, and no builder holds them.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-model`>>

!! The model ~ the five production invariants (the keel)

Six production parsers (Roslyn · TypeScript · rust-analyzer · Babel · Lezer · Tree-sitter) converge on
five invariants — independent of hand-written vs table-generated:

<<~Inv 1-always-a-tree "the parse CONTRACT changes from `tree | throw` to `tree + diagnostics[]` (Babel's `errorRecovery`). Never throw, never null. matklad: this is the whole game — every 'break badly' today is a thrown/aborted parse">>
<<~Inv 2-error-missing-nodes "ERROR + MISSING nodes ARE the gradient primitive — present-but-wrong (ERROR, wraps the unparsable span) vs expected-but-gone (MISSING, zero-width inserted). Everything between clean and garbage is expressible">>
<<~Inv 3-lossless "lossless / full-fidelity trees — the ERROR node holds the bytes VERBATIM, so concatenating the tree reproduces source. This IS our verbatim+AST drawer; the error span lands in the tree, not the floor">>
<<~Inv 4-untyped-typed "a homogeneous untyped tree underneath (every node a kind tag) + typed views on top — so error nodes live ANYWHERE without special-casing every rule">>
<<~Inv 5-bounded-recovery "bounded recovery: recovery-SETS (tokens that signal 'parent resumes here') + the ≥1-char PROGRESS INVARIANT (a skip-loop MUST consume ≥1 char) — kills both hard-fail AND the infinite-loop failure mode">>

''The theory name (it already exists):'' ISLAND GRAMMARS + Water/Lake (Moonen, //Generating Robust//
//Parsers Using Island Grammars//, WCRE 2001 — "Water" = the non-terminal that swallows non-islands;
arXiv 2010.16306 adds "Lakes" = tolerant regions inside islands). The `meme-ast` regex-islands +
Text-gap parser forms an island grammar. It carries a per-node recovery grade (`standing`, 0–20 on
the `aperture` ladder, set only on a recovered node) + an explicit `ErrorNode` (à la
tree-sitter/Lezer/Roslyn). Diagnostics ride OUT-OF-BAND, span-keyed (rust-analyzer's model), on
`ParseMemeResult.failures` — the gradient-annotation drawer over verbatim.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/engine-verdict`>>

!! Engine verdict ~ steal the model, don't swap the engine

TW5's parser runs HAND-WRITTEN rule recursion — so the generated-GLR families offer the wrong SHAPE
to copy; RESILIENT RECURSIVE DESCENT sets the gold pattern (Roslyn / rust-analyzer / matklad's
//Resilient LL Parsing Tutorial//, the canonical modern reference).

* ''Tree-sitter'' — STEAL its `ERROR`/`MISSING`/`error-cost` node model (the cleanest gradient
  formalization, maps 1:1 onto our ladder). REJECT the engine: no TW5-wikitext grammar exists (author
  from zero), markup RESISTS tree-sitter (its own markdown maintainers call it correctness-unsafe +
  needs a C external scanner), recovery runs as an UN-TUNABLE BLACK BOX (fights "steer the
  degradation"), C toolchain in a TS/WASM shop. (WASM-in-3-runtimes actually PASSES — not the blocker.)
* ''Lezer'' — adopt the never-fail discipline + `@lezer/markdown`'s hand-written-incremental
  architecture (BlockParser/InlineParser/NodeSpec) as a template IF we ever build editor-grade. NOT a
  render-parser replacement (its CST ≠ TW5's attribute-rich widget tree; error nodes carry NO
  confidence — the gradient stays ours to derive). NOTE: BurningTreeC shipped a WORKING Lezer wikitext
  parser (CM6 plugin, Jan 2026) — reuse it EDITOR-side, don't rebuild.
* ''Chevrotain'' — the strongest JS-native error-recovery ENGINE (pure-JS, no codegen/WASM, built-in
  recovery; `recoveredNode` + `parser.errors[]` + `recoveryValueFunc` = the graded API) — but adopting
  it = REPLACE (rebuild the widget pipeline). Hold as the escalation option only. (Langium = its LSP
  upgrade; ANTLR loses on Java codegen; nearley/ohm/peggy = weak recovery for MALFORMED input.)

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/architecture`>>

!! The lift architecture ~ AUGMENT + WRAP, reject REPLACE

''REJECT REPLACE'' (re-host wikitext on Lezer/tree-sitter/Chevrotain): violates plugin-not-fork (TW5's
widget render pipeline BONDS to the core WikiParser's `{type,attributes,children}` tree shape —
swapping the parser = re-implementing the widget tree = forking `$:/core`) + isomorphism (a WASM/
non-iso dep fights the node/browser/cli/worker quad our island layer runs in) + throws away a working,
island-shaped parser.

''AUGMENT'' the `meme-ast/` island layer (an island grammar from the start, so the lift stays purely
additive). STANDS in `types.ts`, `builder.ts` and `parse.ts`:
# an ''`Error` node kind'' in `MemeAstKind` (`ErrorNode`: `reason`, `recoveredAs`, `standing`, `pos`,
   `raw`, and the verbatim span in `content`) — the builder emits it for an orphan close; a mis-nest
   and an unclosed frame at EOF force-close MARKED (`recoveredAs`, `standing`), never silently.
# ''`standing: number`'' on `MemeAstBase` (0–20 aperture ladder), set ONLY on a recovered node —
   orphan close 2 · force-closed frame 9 (2 where the sigil declares water) · partial form 13; a clean
   node carries none.
# every recovery recorded out-of-band on `ParseMemeResult.failures`.
UNBUILT: ''phrase-level recovery'' — bounding a force-closed frame at the next blank line / next
opener instead of swallowing to EOF. `findCloseEnd` (`lar-sigil-shared.ts`) returns `null` where no
closer arrives, and the builder's EOF sweep closes the frame over the rest of the document.

''WRAP'' for full TW5 core wikitext. STANDS: the fork's core `WikiParser` keeps the recovery receipt
itself (#/the-tower), and `MemeticParser` appends the sigil recoveries to that one `diagnostics` array
(`failuresToDiagnostics` in `meme-ast/diagnostics.ts`), so a memetic carrier reports an unclosed
emphasis delimiter and an unplaceable sigil on one channel, graded on one ladder. UNBUILT: a
whole-parser tolerant guard as a plugin. The mechanism it would ride: TW5 builds its parser registry
in `wiki.js` via `forEachModuleOfType("parser", … $tw.Wiki.parsers[f] = module[f])` —
''LAST-REGISTERED WINS'' on a duplicate content-type, and plugin modules load AFTER core. So a plugin
`module-type: parser` that does `exports["text/vnd.tiddlywiki"] = TolerantWikiParser` (subclass the
core `WikiParser` and run `parse()` inside a tolerant guard that catches throws + downgrades the
malformed span to a graded `Error`/Text node) ''OVERRIDES core for ALL standard wikitext'' — the way
`memetic-parser.ts` registers `MemeticParser` for the memetic type, aimed at the default type.
Panic-mode-to-water at the WHOLE-parser boundary, zero `$:/core` edits. The same last-wins rule
reaches FINER per-rule recovery: a `module-type: wikirule` exporting an existing rule name shadows it
(`createClassesFromModules` keys rule classes by name). No `TolerantWikiParser` stands in source.

''THE REUSABLE MODULE (the operator's "lift complexity into another module") — UNBUILT:'' extract a
tolerant-parse substrate — `meme-ast/recover.ts` (or `@lararium/parse-substrate`) — exporting:
Water/Island/Lake segmentation · `ErrorNode` + grade constructors · recovery policies
(`syncToNextOpener`, `boundAtBlankLine`, `forceCloseGraded`) · a ''`withRecovery(parseFn)`'' guard
wrapping any throwing parser into a graded result. `builder.ts` · `scanner.ts` · the `MemeticParser`
wrap all consume this ONE module. The rule classes NEVER learn recovery — the substrate owns it
(matklad: recovery lives in the driver, not the grammar). Clean upstream-plugin boundary. The recovery
helpers (`errorNode`, `markRecovered`) live inside `buildMemeAst`.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/tw5-break-modes`>>

!! TW5's actual break-badly modes (grounded, file and symbol)

Three layers, failing differently:
* ''Layer A — `meme-ast/` (island, regex + Text water):'' never throws, and records each recovery
  (`builder.ts`): an orphan close lands as a water `Error` node holding its verbatim span; a mis-nest
  force-closes the frames between, each marked `repaired`; a near-miss sigil the generic catch-all scan
  recognizes grades `missing` (the partial rung) instead of vanishing; an unknown sigil lands as a
  `Dynamic` node of kind `unknown`. Two breaks stand: an unclosed frame SWALLOWS the rest of the doc
  before the EOF sweep force-closes it (marked and recorded, though the tree shape stays a guess), and
  `safeRegex` drops a sigil's scan in silence where its pattern fails to compile (`scanner.ts`).
* ''Layer B — the wikirule (`lar-sigil.ts`):'' a block opener with no closer matches as the opener
  alone, its would-be body parsing on as ordinary wikitext, with no diagnostic recorded; a generic
  `<<~` form no specific rule renders → `__literal__` water — a registered sigil as plain literal
  text, an unknown form as a marked span (`partial` · `water`) with an `unrecognized-sigil-…`
  diagnostic.
* ''Layer C — the standard `$:/core` WikiParser'' (`MemeticParser` only filters its rule classes and
  appends diagnostics; the engine runs unchanged): a regex-rule pump where every parse "succeeds" by
  consuming all input. An unterminated construct consumes-to-EOF or mis-structures; the fork's rules
  record a diagnostic for it (#/the-tower) and grade no node. A throw at RENDER stays uncontained.

Edge hooks: `PranalaEdge` carries `confidence: number | null` (`types.ts`), written `null` by the
edge projection (`edges.ts`), beside `PranalaEdgeViolation{severity}` — the gradient vocabulary on
EDGES, populated by nothing; nodes carry theirs as `standing` + `recoveredAs`.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/recovery-policy`>>

!! Recovery policy ~ RESOLVED: water-floor, repair-as-gated-upgrade (2026-06-27)

The choice never stood as water VERSUS repair: ''water holds the floor, repair arrives as a
per-construct upgrade.''

''LOCK — the WRAP guard ships PANIC-TO-WATER first.'' On any malformed span: catch, wrap the VERBATIM
bytes in a graded `Error` node (`recoveredAs: "water"`, low/raw confidence), parse cleanly around it.
Four reasons water holds the floor: (1) it CAN'T regress — water never forms a wrong tree (verbatim
preserved, zero fabricated structure); (2) it keeps the gradient HONEST — it marks "structure
unknown," it cannot lie, where a mis-repair claims structure it guessed wrong (the
confidently-incorrect-tree failure this whole design kills); (3) it COMPOSES — repair adds on top,
purely additive; (4) it ships cheap (a catch + downgrade vs the per-construct repair tail).
`lar-sigil.ts`'s `__literal__` move does exactly this, scaled here to the parser boundary.

''Repair follows, GATED.'' Add phrase-level repair (insert the MISSING token, resync to a
recovery-set, re-parse) ONLY per-construct, ONLY where the fix reads UNAMBIGUOUS and FREQUENT — an
unclosed `}}}` / `<<~>>` makes a safe insert; a broken table row makes a risky guess and stays water. A
repaired node carries `recoveredAs: "repaired"` at MID (synthesis) confidence — honestly weaker than a
clean parse, honestly stronger than water. The `recoveredAs` field carries the shore: `"water"`,
`"repaired"` and `"missing"` (the partial rung) stand in `types.ts`, and the builder grades a
force-closed frame `repaired`; token-inserting repair stands UNBUILT, landing per-construct. The
gradient stays truthful end to end: clean=canon · repaired=synthesis · water=raw.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/sigil-self-defined-gradient`>>

!! Self-defining sigil failure-gradients ~ #has cap-stacks (3-spirit swarm, 2026-06-27)

The meme-ast SIGIL builder (our island layer, distinct from the TW5 render boundary) hardens into a
RESILIENT DRIVER that READS each sigil's OWN declared failure-gradient — a sigil self-defines how //it//
degrades, via the EXISTING `#has` cap-stack. Two prior-art realms + our ground converged on one design.

''THE CONVERGENCE.''
* GRAMMAR side (SDF3 permissive-grammars [de Jonge/Kats/Visser] · PEG labeled-failures [+2025 semi-auto]
  · matklad Resilient-LL · tree-sitter MISSING/ERROR · Ohm grammar-as-object): recovery lives in a
  resilient DRIVER that READS per-entity declarations — the HYBRID. matklad's objection targets error
  PRODUCTIONS (grammar alternatives that rot + change the accepted language), NOT per-entity recovery
  HINTS the driver reads. Auto-derive each rule's recovery from its own production by default; let the
  entity override.
* COMPOSITION side (ECS behavior-by-having · ocap attenuation [Miller] · algebraic effect-handlers ·
  OTP supervisor-trees · decorator/Tower/resilience4j): the per-entity declaration = an ORDERED
  cap-stack (DATA); ONE dispatcher walks it as a nearest-handler CASCADE; ORDER = the gradient; each
  lower rung an ATTENUATION (strictly weaker); a mandatory inert "water" rung at the floor; resilience4j
  SHIPS this exact shape.
* OUR GROUND: ''`#has` ALREADY runs as an ordered cap-stack'' — `has-stack.mem` (the Seven Clauses,
  ratified 2026-06-12) + `island-caps.ts` (`composeIsland`: onSignal first-wins, ORDER = precedence,
  teardown LIFO). island-caps' first-wins-by-order enacts the nearest-handler cascade the
  effect-handler prior art names. We EXTEND the substrate to grammar entities; we do NOT invent it.

''THE SOUNDNESS INVARIANT (load-bearing):'' a sigil declares its failure-gradient ONLY as recovery that
fires AFTER a parse failure — NEVER as a normal grammar alternative. The clean grammar's accepted
language stays unchanged (the permissive grammar forms a SUPERSET used only for recovery).

''THE 4-RUNG GRADIENT (declared as data; each rung = named prior art):'' (1) clean — normal parse · (2)
partial — minimal-edit completion (tree-sitter MISSING; declare a completion template / required
trailers) · (3) degraded — skip-to-resync, wrap in an Error node (recovery/FOLLOW set; matklad/ANTLR) ·
(4) inert — island/water, opaque span, parse around it (SDF3 water; MANDATORY floor). DEFAULT:
auto-derive from the sigil's own open/close pattern (FIRST → start; FOLLOW → resync) so an un-annotated
sigil degrades for free. OVERRIDE: a declared recovery cap-stack (the `#has` mechanism) / `lar-recover-*`
fields.

''THE DESIGN.'' The sigil tiddler `#has` a recovery cap-stack (the 4 rungs) as DATA. The hardened
builder = the resilient DRIVER: at each failure site it CONSULTS the sigil's gradient and WALKS the
cascade (first rung that can answer), enacting the MECHANISM (minimal-edit · skip-to-resync · water).
Driver owns the mechanism; the sigil declares the policy. Out-of-band failure record on
`ParseMemeResult.failures` (span-keyed — tree stays clean + carries Error/MISSING nodes; diagnostics
ride beside, rust-analyzer-style + our drawer).

''GOLDEN PRACTICES:'' order = the gradient (declared, not emergent — our has-stack order=precedence
already satisfies this) · the inert water rung MANDATORY · attenuation-monotonic (each lower rung
weaker — ocap) · resumable-vs-abortive tagged per rung (effect-handler discipline; resume = degraded,
abort = inert) · the gradient as DATA, the cascade in ONE driver · clean path free of defensive tangle
("let it crash" into the driver).

''THE BUILD.'' STANDS: `SigilRule.recoverAs` (`"water" | "repaired"`), read by `grammar-cache.ts`
from the sigil tiddler's `lar-recover-as` field (no shipped sigil tiddler sets it); the builder
consults it at ONE site, the unclosed frame at EOF (`builder.ts`), where it sets the mark and the
standing — a `"water"` declaration keeps the force-closed structured node; `parseMemeText` threads every recovery out on `ParseMemeResult.failures`
(`parse.ts`), and `failuresToDiagnostics` reads them onto the core parse-diagnostics contract.
UNBUILT: an inert verbatim-water rung for a declared sigil · the orphan-close and unknown-sigil
sites consulting a declared gradient · scanner per-sigil error-accumulation (`scanner.ts`) · the
recovery cap-stack itself (`has-stack`/`island-caps` extended to grammar entities).

<<~/ahu>>

